WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:18.680
You know, what I do is write for children.

00:18.680 --> 00:24.760
I'm probably America's most widely read children's author, in fact, and I always tell people

00:24.760 --> 00:28.060
that I don't want to show up looking like a scientist.

00:28.060 --> 00:34.960
You can have me as a farmer or in leathers, and no one has ever chose farmer.

00:34.960 --> 00:39.980
I'm here today to talk to you about circles and epiphanies, and you know, an epiphany

00:39.980 --> 00:42.820
is usually something you find that you drop someplace.

00:42.820 --> 00:47.660
You just got to go around the block to see it as an epiphany.

00:47.660 --> 00:52.120
That's a painting of a circle, a friend of mine did that, Richard Ballingbroke.

00:52.120 --> 00:56.060
It's the kind of complicated circle that I'm going to tell you about.

00:56.060 --> 01:03.340
My circle began back in the 60s in high school in Stowe, Ohio, where I was the class queer.

01:03.340 --> 01:09.860
I was the guy beaten up bloody every week in the boys' room until one teacher saved

01:09.860 --> 01:10.900
my life.

01:10.900 --> 01:16.840
She saved my life by letting me go to the bathroom in the teacher's lounge.

01:16.840 --> 01:22.820
She did it in secret, she did it for three years, and I had to get out of town.

01:22.900 --> 01:31.740
I had a thumb, I had $85, and I ended up in San Francisco, California, met a lover, and

01:31.740 --> 01:38.900
back in the 80s found it necessary to begin work on AIDS organizations.

01:38.900 --> 01:43.380
About three or four years ago, I got a phone call in the middle of the night from that

01:43.380 --> 01:48.100
teacher, Mrs. Poston, who said, I need to see you.

01:48.100 --> 01:52.700
I'm disappointed that we never got to know each other as adults.

01:52.700 --> 01:58.700
Would you please come to Ohio and please bring that man that I know you have found

01:58.700 --> 02:01.340
by now?

02:01.340 --> 02:08.540
And I should mention that I have pancreatic cancer, and I'd like you to please be quick

02:08.540 --> 02:09.940
about this.

02:09.940 --> 02:13.500
Well, the next day we were in Cleveland.

02:13.500 --> 02:19.640
We took a look at her, we laughed, we cried, and we knew that she needed to be in a hospice.

02:19.680 --> 02:25.400
We found her one, we got her there, and we took care of her and watched over her family

02:25.400 --> 02:30.160
because it was necessary, it's something we knew how to do.

02:30.160 --> 02:36.320
And just as a woman who wanted to know me as an adult got to know me, she turned into

02:36.320 --> 02:41.000
a box of ashes and was placed in my hand.

02:41.000 --> 02:48.320
And what had happened was the circle had closed, it had become a circle.

02:48.480 --> 02:51.920
And that epiphany I talked about presented itself.

02:51.920 --> 02:55.840
The epiphany is that death is a part of life.

02:55.840 --> 03:02.760
She saved my life, I and my partner saved hers.

03:02.760 --> 03:06.440
And you know, that part of life needs everything that the rest of life does.

03:06.440 --> 03:11.880
It needs truth and beauty, and I'm so happy it's been mentioned so much here today.

03:11.880 --> 03:22.880
It also needs dignity, love, and pleasure, and it's our job to hand those things out.

03:22.880 --> 03:23.880
Thank you.

03:23.880 --> 03:24.880
Thank you.

03:24.880 --> 03:25.880
Thank you.

03:25.880 --> 03:26.880
Thank you.

03:26.880 --> 03:27.880
Thank you.

03:27.880 --> 03:28.880
Thank you.

03:28.880 --> 03:29.880
Thank you.

03:29.880 --> 03:30.380
Thank you.

